WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our next guest is
Emmy-nominated for her work

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.871
as Rebecca on the critically
acclaimed series "Ted Lasso."

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:07.407
The second season is out now,
with new episodes

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:10.477
available Friday on Apple TV+.
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:14.147
-Mom!
There are two white people

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:17.716
at the door,
and they're smiling.

00:00:17.716 --> 00:00:19.985
-Can I help you?
-Good afternoon.

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:24.991
We work for Santa Claus,
and I believe he received this.

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:27.460
-My letter!
-Yes, we're terribly sorry

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.829
that your presents
didn't arrive last night.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.563
But as elves,

00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:34.367
we've been personally directed
by Mr. and Mrs. Claus

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.335
to deliver your presents
this afternoon

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:39.138
and to apologize for the delay.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:41.040
-Please welcome to the show
Hannah Waddingham.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.910
How are you, Hannah?
-Hello, hello!

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.212
-It's so -- It's so lovely
to speak to you.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:48.848
I'm such a fan of your work
on the show,

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.384
and congratulations
on your Emmy nomination.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.353
I think the show was expected
to receive some nominations.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:55.688
It got 20.

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:58.825
Were you -- Did you have
any expectations on the day

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.160
that this was going to happen?

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.828
-Oh, my God.
I had no expectations at all.

00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:04.831
In fact, my manager was like,
"We'll get together,

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.832
we'll have breakfast."
I was like, "No, we won't.

00:01:06.832 --> 00:01:07.967
I'm gonna be in bed.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:10.569
I'm not acknowledging
the day at all."

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:12.404
And then he banged on my door
and I was like,

00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:13.873
"You didn't get the memo!"

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.108
And he just said, "Well,
you've all been nominated."

00:01:16.108 --> 00:01:20.846
And that, I think, is so cool,
because we're great pals.

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:23.115
And that would have been
a little awks

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:24.316
if some of us hadn't.
-Yes.

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:26.485
Surely, there would've been
some awks there.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:27.754
And I want to say that I think

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.755
one of the nicest things
about reading about the show

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:31.991
as it goes into
the second season,

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:34.560
because it's a show
that people are drawn to

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.996
because of the optimism
and the kindness,

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.431
it genuinely does seem like

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:40.200
you all like each other
a great deal.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.368
-Yeah, we're a bit --
we're a bit nauseatingly

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:45.037
in love with each other.
We really are.

00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:48.140
It's a little bit gag-worthy,
but it's genuine.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.044
And we just -- I think because
we're all that little bit older

00:01:51.044 --> 00:01:52.311
and we've all been
knocking about

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:54.713
for quite a while,
we all dig each other

00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:57.150
because we've been enjoying
each other's work for years.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.686
So, yeah. I mean,
journalists are always like,

00:02:00.686 --> 00:02:01.854
"Is there not, like,
a little...?"

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:03.522
and you're like,
"No, no, no, no, there isn't.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:05.891
We just genuinely think
each other's quite cool."

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:08.761
-Do you ever find yourself
enjoying the work

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.330
of your colleagues
while you're in a scene?

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.965
-On a take, yeah, yeah.
It's a nightmare.

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:15.334
Like, particularly,
people like Nick Mohammed.

00:02:15.334 --> 00:02:17.670
I just find myself
gawping like a fangirl

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.105
and wanting to take notes.

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:20.540
And, in fact,
he and I have got to the point

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.809
where we make each other laugh
so much that I have to just

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.711
slightly look off, like,
to his earlobe.

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:27.914
[ Laughter ]
Not good.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:31.284
-You have one of the more
difficult roles because you are

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:33.485
the maybe --
outside of maybe Coach Beard,

00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:35.088
you are the most stoic
of the characters.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.189
So it's really a bit unfair
that you have to be

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:38.724
with people who are so funny.

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:39.958
I know.
Well, the worst thing is,

00:02:39.958 --> 00:02:42.395
also having quite a giant mouth
in real life,

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:46.299
I have to try and keep,
you know, contained.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:48.133
And if they make me laugh
in a scene --

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:50.869
like, when Jason's doing
his, "Right leg, left leg,

00:02:50.869 --> 00:02:53.939
better than the first,"
I'm just like, "You are an ass."

00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:57.276
Just, like,
really hard to not laugh.

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:59.345
You know, she's quite like
you said -- she's very stoic,

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:03.316
and I couldn't be less
like that in life. So, yeah.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:06.452
-You obviously are English,
but you would admit to the fact

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:07.920
that you are not a football fan,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.389
or were not
going into this show.

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:12.825
-Not a clue.
Hell no.

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.594
But I think that's good
because, you know,

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:16.795
Rebecca is not meant to know
anything about it, either.

00:03:16.795 --> 00:03:18.965
So I was being method.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:21.400
-And so it's very nice that you
can turn over the expertise

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:22.701
to some of the writers
on the show

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:24.036
when it comes to football.
But I've also heard

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.773
you've had to step
in a few times and correct

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:31.411
maybe the American writing
for an English character.

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:34.280
-Yeah, which I was nervous
about doing, at first.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.249
I literally felt like
I was Mary Poppins on set,

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:40.018
going, "That is not how we speak
the Queen's English, boys."

00:03:40.018 --> 00:03:42.188
But they were -- they were
really like -- you know,

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.623
if at the end
of a sentence, Rebecca says,

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:46.358
"Oh, I don't know. I guess,"
I'm like, "No, no."

00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:48.294
-[ Laughs ]
-"No."

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:49.428
But they're used to it now,
'cause I'm just like,

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:50.496
"She wouldn't say that."

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.365
Or that another character
wouldn't.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:53.833
Brett Goldstein does the same.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:57.637
Juno is, like, somewhere lost
over the Atlantic,

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:00.807
so she doesn't know
who says what where anymore.

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.007
She's like,
"Would I not say that?"

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:03.509
I'm like, "No, you're
from Somerset, woman.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:07.613
No, you wouldn't."
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:09.349
-It's really --
It's really wonderful.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:12.518
And you came from
a theater background.

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:15.754
And I know from being friends
with a lot of the guys

00:04:15.754 --> 00:04:17.556
on the show,
they are both theater fans,

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:19.558
they're both
musical-theater fans.

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:21.794
Do you feel sort of
extra appreciated

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:25.465
knowing that you're around
these sort of West End fanboys?

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:27.567
-Well, that's exactly
what they are.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:30.637
In fact, I said to Jason
and to Brett

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:33.672
and to our lovely
Coach Beard, Brendan,

00:04:33.672 --> 00:04:35.374
I was like, "You do realize
that, in the West End,

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.343
we would call you
West End Wendys."

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.077
Because they know
more show tunes than I do.

00:04:39.077 --> 00:04:40.846
I'm meant to be the one
that's had like a 20-year

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:42.315
musical-theater career,

00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:44.783
and they're suddenly finishing
each other's sentences.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:46.919
I'm kind of sure
that's how I got the role

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.054
because they're all just like,

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:50.189
"Ooh.
I saw you in "Into the Woods."

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:54.293
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:56.795
Brett Goldstein --
I'm telling you, Brett Goldstein

00:04:56.795 --> 00:04:59.398
was fangirling like a bitch,
and he knows that was true,

00:04:59.398 --> 00:05:00.967
so I'm not even speaking
out of turn.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:02.802
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:06.472
-Now, I think this is
a great credit to your

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:09.475
range as an actor
that I did not know this.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:12.044
And I'm aware now that a lot
of people maybe did know this,

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:14.480
that you were in
"Game of Thrones."

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.116
And I mean, again,
Rebecca's such a glam character

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:20.653
that I sort of forgive myself
for not realizing

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:23.455
that you were
in "Game of Thrones."

00:05:23.455 --> 00:05:26.492
You were the shame...
-Looking so, so pretty.

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.261
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:30.629
Do you know who I look like?
Jar Jar Binks.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:35.001
-Yeah. It's not --
They did you no favors

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:36.702
with Septa Unella.

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:38.704
Did you even have a makeup
person on "Game of Thrones"?

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:40.773
-I did. I did.
And they were like,

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:43.275
"Let's add eyebrow hairs
where you would take them out.

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:45.744
Let's stipple her cheeks,
and chuck her out the door."

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:47.713
But the good thing was,
you could fall asleep for a good

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:49.214
four hours in your trailer,
and it didn't matter.

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:50.683
You could look like
you'd been dug up

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:52.585
and get on camera,
and they'd love it.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.854
[ Laughter ]
-Now, is this something --

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.156
Because it is
such a meme-worthy character,

00:05:57.156 --> 00:06:00.626
is this something that gets sent
your way with regularity?

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:03.962
-I get sent things like that,
especially Halloween,

00:06:03.962 --> 00:06:07.666
I get sent, mostly guys,
which I think's hilarious,

00:06:07.666 --> 00:06:10.836
looking all solemn
in the Jar Jar Binks gear,

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:12.938
but they have, like,
blowup dolls

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:16.976
attached to their front
with all kinds of nasties.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.978
[ Both laugh ]

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:20.847
And I never know --
I'm like, "Thank you?"

00:06:20.847 --> 00:06:23.482
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:23.482 --> 00:06:25.517
I think "thank you" is probably
the most -- that's the best --

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:27.286
-Yeah, thanks with
a question mark.

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:29.488
-It's the safest answer
to anything

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:33.559
that could be taken as praise.
So lovely to meet you.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.528
Congratulations
on the well-deserved Emmy,

00:06:36.528 --> 00:06:38.564
and hopefully,
we will see you here soon.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:39.898
-Can I just tell you something,
Seth, quickly?

00:06:39.898 --> 00:06:41.901
-Please.
-Can I tell you that I was --

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:46.138
when I was on Broadway
in 2008 with "Spamalot,"

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:48.274
I discovered you
for the first time,

00:06:48.274 --> 00:06:51.010
and I might have had just a
gentle crush on you ever since,

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.645
so I'm very pleased to meet you,
my friend.

00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:54.246
-Well, that is lovely to hear.

00:06:54.246 --> 00:06:56.481
And let me just say, you were
wonderful in "Spamalot."

00:06:56.481 --> 00:06:59.485
-Thanks so much!
-This is really --

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:00.953
And we're gonna leave --
Make sure we don't cut

00:07:00.953 --> 00:07:02.654
any of this flirty stuff
at the end.

00:07:02.654 --> 00:07:06.225
[ Laughter ]
Keep all this flirty stuff.

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:08.160
New episodes of "Ted Lasso"
are available

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.695
Fridays on Apple TV+.

